+-----------------------------------------------------------------------
| Running ${PKGSTEM} on OpenBSD
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------

This port uses the host backend. Commands run with the invoking user's
ordinary permissions. The optional AgentFS and nono backends are not
provided or validated by this port; no OpenBSD command sandbox is claimed.
The optional Starlark metaskills require an unavailable Go c-shared build.

LiteLLM is pinned to a packaged bugfix release in the same LTS series as
Swival's upstream pin. Swival uses LiteLLM's internal APIs; keep both
packages aligned when upgrading. The port retains a JSON nesting limit in
the Python bridge to bound decoder recursion.

The optional A2A server listens on 127.0.0.1 by default. An explicit
--serve-host can expose it remotely; configure --serve-auth-token and a
trusted TLS endpoint before doing so. Remote requests can invoke this
agent's tools with the server user's permissions. The host backend does
not confine those tools or filter inherited credential variables.

Loopback-bound A2A listeners reject foreign Host and Origin headers,
cross-site browser requests, and non-JSON content types. These checks are
not authentication. Use a bearer token when local clients or web content
are untrusted. An explicit remote bind retains its configured host policy;
its administrator must protect it with authentication and trusted TLS.

Loopback bind classification recognizes IP literals (including mapped
loopback IPv4 addresses in IPv6 form) and OpenBSD's reserved localhost
names. Request Host values retain the standard loopback allowlist plus
the configured local name or address. Unrelated localhost subdomains
are not trusted. These checks do not resolve DNS or enable unsupported
binds. Other configured hostname aliases retain the administrator's
remote-bind policy even if name service resolves them to loopback.
Configure a bearer token for those aliases.
